A||||4||Patrick's Dad||Copyright...My two cents.||||2||
Z||000000||Patrick's Dad||12-28-2001||10:03 PM||||Recently, I've noticed a bump in the number of people who wish to copy a published work, or parts of a (or several) works, either for themselves or for the benefit of the Forum. This may simply be a perception on my part, but I'm here fairly regularly. I like you guys (and girls, too).   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>As a photographer and a writer, I have difficulties with copying a work that is owned by another entity. Personally, I would have quite a bit of difficulty if it were my work being copied, and would pursue the issue as far as I was able to.<P>Don't take that as a threat, but simply as a statement of ownership and protection. The point to that statement is to express my right, then, possibly, to expand the gentle readers' view that a large company (like Disney or General Motors) employs a rather large contingent of attorneys to watchdog this issue, and, as needed, pitbull it for them.<P>The reason is this: If your company existed for a time, and had built up a reputation of being family friendly, or reliable, or whatever, you would go to great pains to protect that image. Goodwill, of course, exists, which is why this Forum may not find itself in danger until one of our self esteemed members ruins it for the rest of us. On the other hand, if else someone were to attempt to make money on your product or service, then you should be paid for your work and creativity. If someone uses your product to besmirch your hard earned reputation, you'd best believe that you would pursue it. Imagine what would happen if someone distributed a film of Mickey Mouse having sex. On a Grand Prix. With Olive Oyl. I think that there would be a serious feeding frenzy in the litigation set.<P>Here's the skinny on Copyright: You may own the book, but that only allows you to carry it with you to the beach or on the train and be entertained or informed. It does not allow you to copy the information and distribute it to someone else, whether for money or free of charge. The same statement goes for artwork or music. Aside from fair use, which allows a student to make copies for a school project or a PAL videotape to get converted to NTSC, the right to copy and distribute that work belongs to the artist and his/her licensees alone. <P>To wit, this is a pretty girl:<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/JenWarm_1.jpg"> <P>This image is copyright 1997 by me. I alone can produce the original negative and model release. No one else can provide legitimate documentation that I have sold or otherwise transferred that right to another entity. By contract, I can make prints and sell them, offer this image to a magazine, or simply put it in my filing cabinet. I can even tell you her name is Gertrude (which it isn't). Those rights belong to me alone. Physically (or electronically) you may do what you will, but it is illegal without my written permission.<P>I have weighed in on a couple of discussions here on the Forum, and, while I haven't been flamed for making my opinion known (much appreciated), I felt that I needed to say these things. Copyright does not only protect the Disneys and the GMs, it protects the little guys, like me.<P>I am all for Capitalism. I think that if you can make it better, faster, cheaper, and can sell it to a willing audience, then by all means. Just be careful that you are not treading on someone else's property, whether physical or intellectual. The law provides some pretty severe penalties. The last thing I'd want to read here is a thread that started, "Lost my house...."<P>For more info:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.loc.gov/copyright/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.loc.gov/copyright/</A> ||||reg||2
Z||000001||jelly2m8||12-28-2001||10:42 PM||||Heres your best bet with your pictures and stuff,<P> Don't post them on the internet if you don't want them copied or used.<P> You know as well as anyone else that somewill use them.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://users.eastlink.ca/~acook1/pegged%2001.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||12-28-2001||10:45 PM||||Photoshop allows you to put an invisible digital watermark on your pics so if you post them on the net you still can prove your copyright.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Patrick's Dad||12-29-2001||10:24 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR> <B>Heres your best bet with your pictures and stuff,<P>Don't post them on the internet if you don't want them copied or used.<P>You know as well as anyone else that somewill use them.</B> <HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That's the point. People will do it, but it's not right. We howl about what we think is right and wrong, but when the chips are down and you're facing a judge, and your opponent is a multi billion dollar corporation....<P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR> <B>Photoshop allows you to put an invisible digital watermark on your pics so if you post them on the net you still can prove your copyright.</B> <HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The example I posted is digimarced, but, like anything (like car alarms) I'm sure that there's a way around it.||||reg||1
